From: Nahum Shalman <nshalman@edgecast.io>
Date: Thu, 5 Jun 2026 18:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Fix FdLock destructor passing illegal LOCK_UN|LOCK_NB to flock

FdLock's destructor calls lockFile(desc, ltNone, false), which routes
through lockFile()'s wait=false branch and produces:

    flock(fd, LOCK_UN | LOCK_NB)

Per POSIX-OFD flock(2) (and the illumos flock(3C) man page), combining
LOCK_UN with LOCK_NB is illegal and returns -1 with errno=EINVAL.

Linux flock(2) is lenient and silently accepts the combination, so this
has been an invisible portability bug for years. illumos correctly
rejects it, which triggers an existing illumos-specific guard at
src/libstore/unix/pathlocks.cc:77 in most cases, but the guard fails
intermittently and surfaces as 'Error 0' SysError crashes from
LocalStore::addTempRoot during any non-trivial build.

The fix: switch the destructor to wait=true. Unlock is non-blocking by
nature on every Unix, so the 'wait' flag is semantically meaningless
for ltNone — but wait=true keeps the LOCK_NB bit out of the operation
flags, producing the legal `flock(fd, LOCK_UN)` call.

This is a portability fix, not an illumos-only workaround. Linux
behavior is unchanged (LOCK_UN with or without LOCK_NB is treated the
same); illumos stops rejecting the call.

---
 src/libstore/include/nix/store/pathlocks.hh | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/src/libstore/include/nix/store/pathlocks.hh
+++ b/src/libstore/include/nix/store/pathlocks.hh
@@ -49,7 +49,7 @@
     ~FdLock()
     {
         if (acquired)
-            lockFile(desc, ltNone, false);
+            lockFile(desc, ltNone, true);
     }
 };
